\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TVerbpattern5
\item[* relevant examples:] in general: DONP\_`compl'
\begin{description}
\item[subrule 1a/b] DONP\_OPENTOSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 force x2 x3: We forced them to cooperate\\
  (vp123): x1 name x2 x3: The President named him to be Secretary of State 
  (Longman)
\item[subrule 2a/b] DONP\_OPENNPPROP\\
  (vp123): x1 elect x2 x3: They elected him President
\item[subrule 3a/b] DONP\_OPENADJPPROP\\
  (vp123): x1 paint x2 x3: He paints the door green\\
Note that the possibility should exist to have an EMPTY in the ADJPPROP: He 
painted the door.
\item[subrule 4a/b1] DONP\_LOCOPENPREPPPROP\\
  (vp123): x1 put x2 on x3: I put the book on the table\\
Note that in these and the following subrules (up to 5b2) the rule is also used 
to disambiguate the function of the PREPPPROP/ADJPPROP: once a locative rule 
has been called on, the value of the actsubcefs is reduced to [loc], etc.
\item[subrule 4a/b2] DONP\_LOCOPEN'ADVPPROP'\\
  (vp012): ??meet x1 x2: The cars met head-on
\item[subrule 5a/b1] DONP\_DIROPENPREPPPROP\\
  (vp123): x1 drive x2 x3: He drove the car into the garage
\item[subrule 5a/b2] DONP\_DIROPEN'ADVPPROP'\\
  (vp123): ?x1 send x2 x3: I sent the boy home
\item[subrule 6a/b] DONP\_EMPTY\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 to EMPTY: He told the whole story\\
Note that it is assumed that this sentence is {\em not\/} derived from `He told 
EMPTY the whole story'. Cf.\ the remarks made under subrule 2 of TVerbpattern4.
\\
Note that in this and the following subrules the EMPTY does not replace a 
complement, but a prepobj (it is assumed that indirect 
objects with `to' or `for' behave just like any other prepobj). Sentences which 
do realise the prepobj are covered by the following transformation, 
TVerbpattern6. To prevent the PREP from having to be spelled out now and deleted 
later in the process in case it is followed by an EMPTY, the subrules covering 
the EMPTY cases are included not in TVerbpattern6, but here in TVerbpattern5.
\item[subrule 7] CLOSEDADJPPROP\_EMPTY\\
  (vp012): seem x1 to EMPTY: He seemed ill\\
 Cf.\ what was said about {\em seem\/} being both a 1-place and a 2-place verb 
under TVerbpattern1, remarks. The XPPROP in subrules 7 and 8 is not an ordinary 
direct object; its relation is not an objrel, but a complrel.
\item[subrule 8] CLOSEDNPPROP\_EMPTY\\
  (vp012): seem x1 to EMPTY: He seemed a fool (Note that `to' is NOT spelled 
out)
\item[subrule 9] OTHERCLOSEDPREPPPROP\_EMPTY\\
  (vp012): seem x1 to EMPTY: He seemed against the proposal (Note that `to' is 
NOT spelled out)
\item[subrule 10a/b] DONP\_OTHEROPENPREPPPROP\\
  (vp123): x1 tear x2 x3: He tore the letter to pieces
\item[subrule 11] THATSENT\_LOCOPENPREPPPROP\\
  (vp012): say x1 x2: It said in the papers that Gorbatshov was killed.
\item[subrule 12a/b:]DONP\_OPENGERUND\\
  (vp123): x1 catch x2 x3: I caught him lying\\
(Note that we have a mapping problem with Dutch (??ik betrapte hem er op dat 
hij loog ; ik betrapte hem op een leugen).
\item[subrule 13a/b:]DONP\_PROSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 force x2 PROSENT: They forced me
\item[subrule 14a/b:]DONP\_QSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 instruct x2 x3: They instructed Reagan how to act like a 
president
\item[subrule 15a/b:]DONP\_THATSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 convince x2 x3: They convinced her that she could not afford it
\item[subrule 16a/b:]DONP\_OPENINFSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 help x2 x3: I helped her clean the windows
\end{description}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule or transformation class) TC:Verbpatternrules
\item[* date of creation:] 18-DEC-1986 11:39:39; 27-NOV-1987
\item[* author:] Agnes Mijnhout, Margreet Sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RVerbpattern5
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 14-DEC-1987\ \ \ \ \ author: Margreet Sanders\\
The disambiguation of the PREPPPROPs and ADVPPROPs in subrules 4 and 5
\item date: 08/06/90\ \ \ \ \ author: Petra de Wit\\
added subrules 12, 13, 14, 15, 16 (for comment see above)
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Checks if the categories of the variables are compatible with the 
categories of the arguments expected by the verb.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] Each subrule 
handles a specific verbpattern.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
values for the attribute "synvps" with basic verbs
\item rules:\\
these transformations follow the startrules.
\end{enumerate}
