\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TVerbpattern6
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}
\begin{description}
\item[subrule 1a/b:] PREPNP\_THATSENT\\
  (vp012): seem to x1 x2: It seems to me that he is ill\\
  (vp123): x1 mention to x2 x3: He mentioned to me that he would be back early
\item[subrule 2a/b:] PREPNP\_QSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 inquire of x2 x3: I inquired of him what he wanted
\item[subrule 3a/b:] PREPNP\_OPENTOSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 sign to/?for x2 x3: The policeman signed to me to stop\\
  It is possible that a construction with {\em for\/} should be analysed as a
  FORTOSENT. Whether in that case {\em sign\/} is still ditransitive (He signed 
  to EMPTY [for me to stop]) or simply monotransitive (He signed [for me to 
stop]) is unclear. Cf. the sentence `He signalled to his officers for the 
attack to begin': Jan Odijk suggested that in this sentence, the part starting 
with {\em for\/} is not an argument, but a {\em doelzin\/}, which can be added 
freely. A final alternative is that {\em for\/} introduces a PREPCLOSEDTOSENT 
argument. Note that neither the PREPNP\_FORTOSENT option nor the PREPNP\_
PREPCLOSEDTOSENT have been provided for in the current rules. 
\end{description}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)TC:
Verbpatternrules
\item[* date of creation:] 18-DEC-1986, 08-DEC-1987
\item[* author:] Agnes Mijnhout, margeet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RVerbpattern6
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 30/08/88\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders, jan odijk:\\
added a condition on the specQ of the PREPP (needed for Lisette in the 
aktionsart rules) and a condition on the identity of aspect, deixis, class and 
retro of PREP and PREPP. SpecQ is assumed yesspec in all cases where the object 
of the PREPP has no specQ value of its own.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Checks if the category of the variables is compatible with the 
restrictions on the categories of the arguments of the verb.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Values for the attribute `synvps'in the dictionary for basic verbs
\item rules:\\
These transformations follow the startrules.
\end{enumerate}
